  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Canadian high pressure will persist ahead of a cold \nfront and accompanying good chance of showers/storms that will \nsettle across cntl NC on Fri and linger mainly across srn/sern NC on \nSat.  \n\nDownstream of an anomalous mid/upr-level ridge now building across \nthe srn Appalachians, Canadian surface high pressure will extend \ndown the Middle Atlantic coast and into the Carolinas, while \ngradually weakening, through early Thursday. A lee trough will \ndevelop over the southern Middle Atlantic Piedmont later Thursday \nand precede a cold front that will settle across the region Friday-\nSaturday. \n\nSeasonable temperatures and low humidity levels will gradually \nmoderate/modify ahead of the cold front. While a shower or storm \nwill become possible over the far nw Piedmont by Thu afternoon-\nevening, the relative best chance of convection will exist along the \npassing cold front throughout cntl NC on Fri, then linger along and \njust behind the front (where moisture/instability will remain) \nmainly across srn/sern NC on Sat. Sat will otherwise not be as hot \nas previously forecast in the post-frontal regime. Return flow Sun \ninto early next week will then yield increasing surface \ntemperatures/instability and slight to low chances for diurnal \nconvection.",
